# `engines` in package.json is a refusal at install time, not a suggestion.
#
# npm's default is to warn — `npm warn EBADENGINE` scrolls past in an install
# that then succeeds — and the version it warned about goes on to fail later with
# no memory of the warning. With this line the same `npm ci` refuses in about
# three seconds, naming the required range and the version in hand.
#
# **What it does not cover is a run**, and that is worth stating here rather than
# leaving as an assumption: npm checks `engines` on install and not on `npm run`,
# measured by setting the range to `^99.0.0`, at which `npm run typecheck` still
# ran to completion and exited 0. So an already-installed tree run under an
# unsupported Node gets nothing from this file. `vite.config.ts` refuses that
# case, at the top, and says why there — the two halves are one guard.
engine-strict=true
